{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:13:10.961966", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_architect_completed", "data": {"problem_analysis": "The core challenge lies in jointly optimizing INA placement and worker-to-aggregator assignment under strict hardware and network constraints. The problem couples discrete decisions (which switches to enable) with continuous flow dynamics (data rates), where poor placement creates hotspots and poor assignment wastes resources. Critical data structures include job-specific worker/PS mappings, precomputed shortest paths, switch processing capacities, and link bandwidths. The solution must respect topology-aware constraints such as Pod-based oversubscription and the 2:1 bottleneck in Fat-Tree networks.", "strategy_overview": "The strategy follows a constructive, multi-stage pipeline: first, a prioritized candidate selection for INA placement based on network centrality and job density; second, a routing assignment that balances load across selected switches while respecting physical constraints; finally, the orchestration of these components into a valid solution format. Each stage builds upon the previous, leveraging existing preprocessing and path-lookup utilities.", "function_count": 3, "functions": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators", "solve_with_heuristic"]}}
