{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:15:19.957288", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by path coverage and hop count to PS failed to account for cross-Pod traffic congestion in the 2:1 oversubscribed Fat-Tree topology, leading to critical saturation of Core layer links (e.g., Aggr(171) → Core(179)) because it prioritized switches that were topologically close to PS without considering the aggregate cross-Pod load. This caused INA placement to be concentrated in a few Pods, exacerbating upstream bottlenecks instead of distributing load. Additionally, the worker assignment heuristic assigned workers greedily to the least-utilized INA switch without enforcing balanced load distribution across Pods or considering the shared core bandwidth, resulting in underutilized ToR switches and overloading of specific links (e.g., Server(52) → ToR(162)) due to poor spatial awareness. The failure to model Pod-level load balancing during assignment led to 'hotspot' aggregation in under-served Pods while other Pods remained idle, violating the fundamental goal of INA: to reduce cross-Pod congestion by localizing aggregation.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
