{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:17:20.059072", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by cross-Pod traffic reduction and job volume failed to account for the asymmetric load distribution across Pods and the physical constraint that INA placement must be balanced across Pod boundaries to avoid hotspot switches. This caused over-concentration of workers on a single ToR switch (e.g., ToR(164)) due to unbalanced assignment, leading to switch processing overload (utilization > 100%) and complete underutilization of PS links (all PSs idle), even though INA was deployed. Additionally, the worker assignment strategy of assigning to the 'least loaded' INA switch without considering the actual switch processing capacity or the PS bottleneck condition led to poor resource utilization and persistent core-layer congestion (e.g., Aggr(174) → Core(179) saturated at 100%). The algorithm prioritized intra-Pod routing but ignored the fact that high-volume jobs spanning multiple Pods still generated excessive cross-Pod traffic when aggregated at a single switch, violating the 2:1 oversubscription rule. The core flaw was treating INA placement and assignment as independent decisions, when in reality, the assignment must be constrained by both the INA switch’s capacity and the global network topology to prevent bottlenecks.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
