{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:19:21.638113", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by cross-Pod load reduction and Pod imbalance penalization failed to account for the critical bottleneck at the PS access link and the resulting underutilization of INA switches. This caused a misalignment between INA placement and actual traffic reduction, as the algorithm prioritized switches that reduced cross-Pod hops but ignored whether the actual gradient volume was being aggregated before reaching the PS. As a result, INA-enabled switches were deployed in suboptimal locations (e.g., Aggr/ToR switches with low worker proximity), while the PS bottleneck remained unaddressed, leading to 100% utilization on PS access links and zero aggregation at PS nodes. Additionally, the worker assignment heuristic of assigning to the least-loaded INA switch in the worker's Pod (or globally) ignored the end-to-end flow conservation constraints and failed to ensure that aggregated flows actually reached the PS within the switch processing limit, resulting in idle INA switches despite high network congestion. This demonstrates that topological scoring without flow-aware routing and end-to-end load balancing leads to poor resource utilization and makespan degradation.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
