{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:21:18.931934", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by topological influence and cross-Pod traffic reduction failed to account for switch processing capacity constraints and PS-level load balancing, leading to severe switch overloads (e.g., ToR(167) at 82.6% utilization, Aggr(172) overloaded) and underutilized PS nodes despite INA deployment, because the selection prioritized path-based metrics over actual resource saturation. Similarly, the worker assignment heuristic of greedily assigning to the least-loaded local INA switch without considering the cumulative traffic load across all jobs caused imbalance, resulting in single switches being assigned too many workers and exceeding their 750.0 Gbps processing limit, while other INA-enabled switches remained idle (e.g., Core(179) with 0.0% utilization). This reveals that purely topological or local load-balancing heuristics cannot ensure global capacity adherence or avoid hotspot formation when job sizes and spatial distributions vary significantly.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
