{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:25:15.452464", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates based on total traffic through a switch, combined with a static pod-balancing heuristic, failed to account for the hierarchical congestion in the 3-tier Fat-Tree topology, leading to over-concentration of cross-Pod traffic on Core-layer links and persistent critical saturation (100% utilization) despite INA deployment. This occurred because the heuristic prioritized high-traffic switches without evaluating whether their placement would route traffic through the most congested bottleneck paths (e.g., Aggr→Core links). Additionally, the worker assignment logic strictly enforced assignment to local INA switches only if capacity allowed, but ignored the fact that cross-Pod aggregation at a well-placed switch could reduce overall Core-layer load by aggregating multiple jobs before they converge. The failure to model inter-POD traffic reduction as a primary objective caused INA switches to be underutilized (often idle) while Core links remained saturated, resulting in wasted INA budget and poor makespan. Furthermore, the assignment logic did not consider the PS link as a viable aggregation point for cross-Pod traffic when INA switches were overloaded, forcing all traffic through the Core, exacerbating the bottleneck.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
