{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:26:12.441930", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of assigning workers to INA switches based solely on Pod-level load balancing and a static ranking of candidates failed to enforce the strict validity constraint that an assignment must only target either the PS or an INA-enabled switch. This caused routing assignments to point to non-INA, non-PS nodes (e.g., Node 167, 175) because the `_assign_workers_to_aggregators` function did not verify whether the candidate switch was actually selected in the placement (i.e., included in the top `ina_budget` switches from `_ina_candidate_ranking`). The core flaw was treating the ranked list as a set of available switches without checking membership in the final placement, leading to invalid assignments that violated the 'assignment validity' constraint (y_{jws} <= x_s). Additionally, the heuristic did not consider that INA switches must be explicitly selected before being used, which caused the assignment logic to assign workers to switches not in the final deployment, resulting in infeasible solutions.", "functions_to_update": ["_assign_workers_to_aggregators", "_select_ina_candidates"]}}
