{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:28:28.831984", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by inverse cross-Pod hop count and normalized influence failed to account for the physical bandwidth hierarchy in Fat-Tree topologies, leading to the selection of Core-layer switches that are inherently bottlenecked by 2:1 oversubscription. This caused INA placement on switches like Core(177), Core(178), and Core(179) to be wasted—despite budget allocation—because they were never used in any worker-to-aggregator path. The core flaw was treating all switches as equally viable for aggregation, ignoring that Core switches are critical cross-Pod chokepoints and cannot efficiently absorb aggregated traffic from multiple Pods. Furthermore, the assignment logic prioritized local INA switches in the same Pod without verifying whether those switches had sufficient downstream bandwidth to handle the aggregated load, resulting in 'phantom' INA deployment: switches were selected but never received traffic. This created a false sense of optimization, where the algorithm appeared to be balancing load, but in reality, it was misallocating the INA budget to high-latency, low-throughput nodes while leaving high-impact, intra-Pod aggregation opportunities unexploited. The outcome was not only wasted budget but also the failure to reduce PS congestion, as the aggregation logic never routed any worker traffic through the INA-enabled switches. The system remained dependent on direct PS uploads, causing 100% saturation on key cross-Pod links like Aggr(170) → Core(177) and Core(177) → Aggr(175), which remained the sole path for all inter-Pod traffic.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
