{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:30:37.370210", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by total traffic volume and cross-Pod influence failed to account for the asymmetric bandwidth constraints of the Fat-Tree topology, particularly the 2:1 oversubscription at Pod boundaries. This caused the algorithm to prioritize switches in high-traffic Pods without considering the actual path congestion, leading to critical link saturation (e.g., ToR(164) → Aggr(172)) and wasted INA budget on switches that were not on any worker-to-PS path. Additionally, the assignment heuristic of sorting aggregators by load while ignoring the physical link capacity and PS link bandwidth (200.0 Gbps) resulted in PS bottlenecks, where multiple jobs were assigned to the same PS despite its last-hop link being saturated, causing 'PS Access Bottleneck' issues. The failure to enforce inter-switch load balancing across the network hierarchy led to overloading specific switches (e.g., ToR(160)) while others remained underused. Finally, the assignment condition 'switch_load[agg_node] + job_volume / workers_per_job < Cs' was too simplistic—it did not consider the cumulative effect of multiple jobs using the same switch, nor did it model the end-to-end flow conservation constraints, resulting in invalid configurations that violated the switch processing capacity constraint. These design choices collectively caused resource underutilization (e.g., INA-enabled switches with zero traffic) and severe congestion at strategic choke points.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
