{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:32:58.128418", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates based solely on total gradient volume passing through a switch, combined with a rigid Pod-based load-balancing heuristic, failed to account for the asymmetric nature of Fat-Tree topology and the critical 2:1 oversubscription bottleneck. This caused the algorithm to concentrate INA placement on switches in Pods with high cross-Pod traffic, inadvertently creating hotspots in the aggregation layer while underutilizing switches in low-traffic Pods. Furthermore, the worker assignment strategy of assigning to the least-loaded INA switch in the same Pod—without considering the end-to-end path length or the PS link capacity—led to poor load distribution across PS nodes, resulting in critical PS last-mile congestion (utilization 100%) and significant underutilization of INA-enabled switches (e.g., utilization < 5% despite budget cost). The algorithm's failure to model the coupling between INA placement and PS link load resulted in a scenario where INA was deployed but ineffective, as the volume reduction was not targeted toward the most congested PS links. This demonstrates that a topological-aware, end-to-end congestion model is essential: INA placement must prioritize switches that reduce the number of worker-to-PS paths traversing the same PS link, not just the volume passing through a switch.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
