{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:35:51.015896", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by total volume and cross-Pod reduction failed to account for the critical 2:1 oversubscription ratio in the Fat-Tree topology, leading to the selection of Core-layer switches (e.g., Aggr(172), Aggr(174)) that are inherently congested and prone to saturation—causing critical link overloads (e.g., ToR(164) → Aggr(172) at 100%) despite INA budget being spent. Additionally, the worker assignment strategy of assigning all workers in a Pod to the least-loaded INA switch, without considering the PS bottleneck or the physical link capacity constraints, resulted in severe PS last-mile congestion (e.g., ToR(166) → Server(134) at 100%) and underutilization of INA switches in high-volume Pods. The algorithm treated INA placement and worker assignment as independent decisions, ignoring the coupling between switch load and link utilization, which caused the system to fail in balancing traffic across the network hierarchy.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
