{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:38:02.566104", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by cross-Pod traffic reduction and pod load imbalance failed to handle heterogeneous job sizes and spatial clustering, leading to poor resource utilization and persistent PS link saturation. Specifically, the score function prioritized switches that reduced cross-Pod congestion but ignored the actual volume of data being aggregated, resulting in INA switches being placed in underutilized pods while high-volume jobs continued to overload PS links. Additionally, the worker assignment logic, which assigned workers only to INA switches within the same Pod as the PS, prevented effective load balancing across multiple INA candidates and forced all traffic from large jobs to be funneled through a single PS link, even when multiple INA switches were available. This caused a critical failure in congestion mitigation, as the algorithm failed to distribute aggregation work across switches in a way that reduced end-to-end flow pressure on the PS bottleneck.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
