{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:40:00.384952", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidate switches solely by total job volume passing through and cross-Pod traffic reduction potential failed to account for Pod-level load imbalance and PS link congestion patterns, leading to suboptimal INA placement that left high-traffic PS links saturated (utilization = 200.00 Gbps) while underutilizing or wasting budgeted INA switches. This occurred because the scoring function did not penalize scenarios where INA placement did not reduce traffic on the critical PS access link (ToR→PS), nor did it prioritize switches that could absorb local Pod traffic. Furthermore, the worker assignment heuristic assigned workers to INA switches only within the same Pod as the PS, which ignored opportunities to leverage inter-Pod INA switches for cross-Pod traffic reduction, resulting in critical congestion on core and aggregation links (e.g., Aggr(174)→Core(179)) and wasted INA capacity on switches not on any worker-to-PS path. The greedy load-balancing within Pod, without considering the actual physical path lengths and link capacities, caused fragmentation and hotspot formation on specific ToR switches (e.g., ToR(164)) while other switches remained idle.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
