{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:42:07.077680", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of selecting INA candidates based solely on aggregate volume and Pod-level cross-Pod reduction failed to account for the hierarchical bandwidth constraints of the Fat-Tree topology, particularly the 2:1 oversubscription at the Aggregation and Core layers. This caused the algorithm to over-prioritize switches in the Core layer (despite their low effective capacity due to oversubscription) and under-prioritize strategically positioned ToR switches in high-traffic Pods, resulting in severe switch overload (e.g., ToR(164) at 100%+ utilization) and underutilized INA budget (e.g., INA-enabled switches with 0% traffic). The heuristic’s failure to model the actual path load distribution—especially the cost of cross-Pod traffic—led to unbalanced assignment where high-volume jobs were routed through congested Core links (e.g., Aggr(172) → Core(178)) while local intra-Pod aggregation was ignored. Furthermore, the assignment logic of assigning workers only to INA switches in the same Pod as the PS created a rigid bottleneck: when no local INA switch was available, all workers were forced to the PS, creating a 'last-mile' incast. This caused PS nodes to remain idle (0% utilization) despite massive data volume, proving that the routing logic failed to exploit INA placement even when deployed. The core failure lies in treating INA placement and assignment as decoupled decisions, when in reality, the routing must be sensitive to the physical path cost and switch processing capacity, not just topological proximity.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
