{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:44:04.145044", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidate switches solely based on topological influence scores and cross-Pod traffic reduction potential failed to account for the physical bottleneck of the PS link (200 Gbps), leading to over-concentration of workers on a single PS node despite INA deployment. This caused critical saturation of the PS access link (ToR → PS) in every scenario, while the selected INA switches remained idle (WASTED BUDGET), indicating that the placement heuristic did not ensure load distribution across PS nodes. Furthermore, the worker assignment logic prioritized switches based on minimal path cost without enforcing a minimum utilization threshold, resulting in a greedy assignment that never routed traffic through INA switches—even when they were valid candidates—because the PS link was perceived as 'cheaper' in the cost model. This created a feedback loop where INA switches were selected but unused, and PS links became saturated due to unbalanced routing. The core failure was a misalignment between the objective (minimizing makespan) and the cost function, which ignored the critical constraint that INA must be used to offload PS traffic.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
