{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:45:59.424273", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of scoring INA candidates based on cross-Pod hop reduction and applying uniform penalties to Core/Aggr switches failed to account for the physical reality that INA must be placed at the ToR layer to effectively reduce PS-level congestion. This caused the algorithm to prioritize high-risk Core and Aggr switches (with penalties) while ignoring ToR switches, which are the only valid aggregation points for intra-Pod traffic and the only location where PS-side congestion can be alleviated. As a result, the algorithm selected INA-enabled switches that were physically disconnected from any worker traffic (e.g., ToR(163), ToR(164)), leading to 'WASTED BUDGET' and persistent PS link saturation at 100%. Furthermore, the worker assignment logic restricted INA assignments to switches within the same Pod as the PS, but failed to enforce that the selected INA switch must be directly connected to the PS's ToR — a critical condition for reducing last-hop traffic. This caused INA switches to be placed in non-adjacent Pods or on non-adjacent links, making them incapable of aggregating traffic before it reaches the PS, thereby nullifying their benefit. The combined effect was that INA was deployed on switches that could not serve any worker due to routing constraints, while PS links remained overloaded, creating a cascading failure in the system's ability to reduce makespan.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
