{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:47:59.706293", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidate switches based on a composite score that heavily rewarded ToR switches connected to the PS's ToR, while penalizing Aggr and Core switches due to 2:1 oversubscription, failed to handle cross-Pod workload imbalance. This caused suboptimal INA placement: switches were chosen based on local Pod proximity to PS, but the algorithm did not account for the fact that INA must be placed on switches that can absorb and aggregate cross-Pod traffic without creating new bottlenecks at the Core or Aggregation layers. As a result, INA was deployed on low-traffic, underutilized ToRs (e.g., ToR(166) with 0% utilization) while high-traffic cross-Pod paths remained unaggregated, causing PS access links to saturate. Furthermore, the worker assignment logic restricted aggregation to only local INA switches in the same Pod as the PS, ignoring the potential of remote INA switches in other Pods to reduce long-haul cross-Pod traffic. This rigid Pod-based assignment exacerbated Core and Aggregation layer congestion, especially when multiple jobs had workers across multiple Pods. The failure was not due to insufficient budget, but due to a misalignment between the placement and assignment logic: INA was placed in locations that could not reduce the dominant bottleneck (cross-Pod traffic), while the assignment logic prevented offloading work to more strategic, higher-impact switches.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
