{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:51:00.014516", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely based on cross-Pod traffic reduction and proximity to PS ToR switches failed to account for pod-level load balancing and switch processing capacity constraints, leading to severe overloading of specific ToR and Aggregation switches (e.g., ToR(161) at 100% utilization) while other INA-enabled switches were left idle (e.g., Aggr(172) with 0% traffic). This imbalance occurred because the heuristic prioritized topological influence without enforcing real-time load-aware assignment, causing a few switches to become stragglers despite being within the budget. Additionally, the worker assignment rule of 'assign to least-loaded INA switch in same Pod' failed to consider the actual processing throughput (Cs = 750 Gbps) and led to overloading of switches that were not capacity-constrained in theory but became bottlenecks in practice due to uneven worker distribution. This demonstrates that topological scoring alone is insufficient—without dynamic load monitoring and capacity-aware routing, even well-placed INA switches become ineffective, and the PS link remains saturated due to unaggregated traffic.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
