{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:53:37.698283", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by total traffic volume passing through a switch, while applying a 50% penalty to Aggr/Core switches and a 50% bonus to ToR switches connected to the PS's ToR, failed to handle workload skew across Pods and ignored the critical 2:1 oversubscription bottleneck in cross-Pod paths. This caused INA placement to concentrate on high-volume ToRs (especially those connected to PSs with many workers), leading to severe PS link saturation (utilization = 100%) because aggregation was not effectively reducing traffic volume at the root of the bottleneck. Simultaneously, the greedy intra-Pod assignment rule in worker routing—restricting aggregation to switches within the same Pod as the PS—prevented cross-Pod load balancing and forced all traffic from a Pod to flow through its PS’s ToR, exacerbating congestion. As a result, INA switches were either underused (wasted budget, e.g., ToR(160) with 0% utilization) or overloaded (e.g., ToR(164) with 46.5%) due to poor spatial alignment, while the PS access links remained saturated. The core failure was a lack of coupling between INA placement and spatial job distribution: the algorithm failed to place INA at strategic points that could absorb cross-Pod traffic before it reached the PS ToR, and the assignment logic did not allow for inter-Pod aggregation even when beneficial.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
