{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:56:15.319412", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates solely by cross-Pod traffic volume and Pod-locality bonuses failed to account for actual PS load distribution and switch processing capacity constraints, leading to inefficient budget allocation. This caused critical switches like ToR(164) and Aggr(172) to be saturated (100% utilization) while others—such as ToR(166) and Aggr(174)—were wasted despite being selected, because the assignment heuristic only considered intra-Pod INA switches and ignored the global flow balance. Furthermore, the worker assignment rule of 'assign to least-loaded valid INA switch in the same Pod' created artificial fragmentation: jobs with workers across multiple Pods could not leverage INA switches in other Pods, even if they were underutilized, and forced all cross-Pod traffic to route through the PS bottleneck, resulting in PS link saturation and wasted INA capacity. This revealed that a purely local, Pod-centric assignment strategy cannot handle global traffic balancing under 2:1 oversubscription, especially when INA deployment is constrained and PS links are already overloaded.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
