{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T13:58:28.833621", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of ranking INA candidates based on total job volume traversing each switch, without considering Pod-specific congestion and cross-Pod traffic costs, failed to prioritize switches that could most effectively reduce PS bottlenecks and cross-Pod congestion. This caused INA placement to favor high-volume switches in non-critical locations (e.g., Core switches), resulting in wasted budget on switches that processed zero traffic. Additionally, the assignment strategy of assigning all workers in a job to a single local INA switch, regardless of that switch’s processing capacity or existing load, led to severe overloading of specific ToR switches (e.g., ToR(161) with 100% utilization), while other INA-enabled switches remained idle. This greedy, Pod-local assignment failed to balance load across available INA nodes and ignored the physical bandwidth hierarchy, especially the 2:1 oversubscription that makes cross-Pod links expensive. As a result, the algorithm created new bottlenecks in the core and aggregation layers, despite INA deployment, and left PS links saturated due to ineffective aggregation. The combination of poor candidate selection and rigid assignment caused INA to be underused, while the system remained constrained by last-mile PS congestion and cross-Pod bottlenecks.", "functions_to_update": ["_select_ina_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
